---
title: The Boundary
summary: Every island is a serialization boundary. import.meta.og.$ is the one mark that carries anything across it — functions included — into context or props. Stores cross automatically; refusals fail at your line, not in a serializer.
related:
  - data-state/state-context
  - macros/wire
  - regions/client-islands
---

Every island is its own hydration root, so anything that travels from a server-rendered host
into an island — a prop, a context value — crosses a **serialization boundary**. Plain data
crosses for free. Everything else needs a way to travel, and ogygia's rule for it is one
sentence:

> **Mark the boundary with `import.meta.og.$()`. Everything that crosses is either data, or
> something that knows how to rebuild itself on the other side.**

## `import.meta.og.$` — the one mark

`og.$` wraps a value at the exact place it heads for a boundary. What it does depends on what
you hand it:

### A function — it crosses *alive*

```svelte
<script>
	import { setContext } from 'ogygia';

	const tax = 0.19; // a local — captured by VALUE

	setContext('fmt', import.meta.og.$((n) => `€${(n * (1 + tax)).toFixed(2)}`));
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- inside any island, plain svelte: -->
<script>
	import { getContext } from 'svelte';
	const fmt = getContext('fmt'); // a REAL, callable function
</script>
<p>{fmt(100)}</p> <!-- €119.00 — synchronous, no eval in production -->
```

The compiler hoists the function, turns each captured local into a bound value that crosses
as data, and the island rebinds it. The function itself is **never serialized** — its code
ships as a build artifact, its captures ship like any prop. Captures follow prop law: they
must serialize (a captured store crosses live and stays shared — see below).

Two things are build errors, at your file:line:

- capturing anything from a **server-only module** (`$env/static/private`, `$app/server`,
  `*.server.ts`) — the value would ship into client HTML;
- marking something that can never cross (a DOM node, a bare function inside an object) —
  the error fires at the mark, not later inside a serializer.

### Anything else — an explicit, checked crossing

```svelte
<Cart
	items={import.meta.og.$(cartStore)}
	onCheckout={import.meta.og.$((id) => `/checkout/${id}`)}
/>
```

For a non-function value, `og.$` is a **boundary assertion**: the value is classified right
there — a store passes (and will auto-wire), plain data passes, a live DOM node or an
un-codec'd class instance **throws at that line** with the path inside the value that
offended. The value itself is returned untouched, so there is no wrapper to unlearn.

You never *need* `og.$` for values that cross on their own (see the table below) — but
marking the boundary is the recommended style: the next reader sees exactly where the page
splits, and mistakes surface at the mark instead of at serialize time.

## What crosses, and how

| You pass | How it travels | Needs a mark? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| plain data, `Map`/`Set`/`Date` | serialized (devalue) | no |
| a **store** (`subscribe`-shaped) | value crosses; islands **reunite to ONE live instance** — `set` in island A repaints island B | no — automatic |
| a **factory store** (custom methods) | value crosses; methods **rebuilt from the factory** on the other side | usually no — provable factories auto-brand; assert with `og.store` (below) otherwise |
| a **class instance** | via its own codec: [`import.meta.og.wire`](/docs/macros/wire) | once, on the class |
| a **function** | hoisted code + bound captures (`og.$`) | yes — `og.$` |
| a **snippet** | freezes to HTML, or crosses live (`{#snippet}`) | no |
| a component / UI | it's a [region](/docs/regions/held-regions) — a different noun on purpose | `region()` / `<Region>` |
| a DOM node, a bare callback in an object, a secret | **refused**, with the path and a fix | — |

## Stores: automatic, shared, live

```svelte
<script>
	import { setContext } from 'ogygia';
	import { writable } from 'svelte/store';
	setContext('theme', writable('dark'));
</script>
```

Every island that reads `getContext('theme')` gets **the same live store** — not a copy. The
current value crosses at render time; identity is reunified client-side, so writes propagate
between islands through plain Svelte reactivity. The same store provided under two keys stays
one instance.

### Factory stores

A store with custom methods needs its **factory** on the other side (methods are code — they
never serialize). Factories the compiler can prove are branded automatically:

```ts
// auto-branded: the body provably returns a store
export const createCart = (seed = []) => {
	const { subscribe, set, update } = writable(seed);
	return { subscribe, set, update, add: (item) => update((x) => [...x, item]) };
};
```

For a factory it can't prove (it returns a helper's result), assert it:

```ts
export const createCart = import.meta.og.store((seed = []) => makeStore(seed));
```

The factory's **first parameter is the seed** — the crossing value. If a branded store
reaches an island whose bundle never loaded the factory, it degrades to a plain writable
(value intact, methods absent) with a console warning naming the one-import fix — never a
crash.

Two store shapes deserve care: a **`derived`** crosses as a frozen seed (the derivation
can't travel — re-derive client-side or share the sources), and store **values** follow prop
law (a callback inside a store's value is still a bare function).

## Keeping things home: `{ islands: false }`

Not everything should cross. Context that only the server-rendered host reads — a bag of
element refs, a host-only callback, a huge config object — stays native with one option:

```js
setContext('scrollRefs', refs, { islands: false });
```

Same-tree `getContext` works as always; nothing is serialized. In dev, ogygia warns when the
island context payload grows past 32kB and names the heaviest keys — this option is usually
the fix.

## Policy and errors

The boundary refuses secret-looking keys (`token`, `apiKey`, …) so credentials don't ride
into client HTML. Tune it once, server-side:

```ts
// hooks.server.ts
import { configure_boundary } from 'ogygia/internal';

configure_boundary({
	allow: ['tokenColor'],   // a false positive the sniff should let through
	deny: ['sessionBlob']    // a semantic secret the sniff can't see
});
```

Everything the boundary declines is **explained**: dropped context keys log the key, the
dot-path to the offending leaf, and what to do instead. The design goal is that nothing
crosses — or fails to cross — silently.
